Start here
==========

This is a Windows port of litmus (https://github.com/notroj/litmus).
If you are picking this up cold, read these in order:

  1. README.md    what this fork is, how to build it, how to run it
  2. AGENTS.md    how to use litmus to test a WebDAV server, and how to
                  read the results; also the JSON schema
  3. PATCHES.md   what was changed inside neon/ and why, needed before
                  you update the bundled neon
  4. This file    what is left to do

Layout:

  src/            the test suites, one .c per suite, plus common.c
  neon/           vendored neon 0.37.1, checked in, not a submodule
  neon/test/common/tests.c
                  the shared harness: result formatting, counters,
                  JSON output. Patched; see PATCHES.md
  win32/config.h  generated config.h, checked in so Makefile.w32 works
                  without autotools
  tests/          test harnesses (wsgidav.sh for Windows and Unix,
                  httpd for Unix only) and expected results
  Makefile.w32    the no-autotools build
  .github/        CI: both build paths, self-test, release

Before you change anything
==========================

Run the baseline and note the numbers:

  ./tests/wsgidav.sh --check

It must print "All suites match the expected results". If it does not,
fix that before doing anything else. Everything below assumes a green
baseline.

Conventions, in code and in prose:

  * Binary units, always: KiB, MiB, GiB, powers of 1024. Never KB, MB
    or GB for a quantity of bytes, and never a decimal power of ten.
    Rates are MiB/s.
  * Timestamps are ISO 8601 in UTC with millisecond precision and a
    trailing Z, e.g. 2026-08-18T13:04:54.429Z. Truncate sub-second
    digits, do not round, so a stamp never names a moment that had not
    happened yet.
  * Durations are seconds to millisecond resolution, and are
    wall-clock. Say so when reporting them, because they include server
    and network time.
  * Quote exact byte counts where the exact figure matters, with the
    rounded binary figure in brackets after it.

If you touch neon/test/common/tests.c or src/common.c, the default text
output must stay byte for byte identical. The way to prove that is to
build the tree twice, once with your change and once without, and diff
the output of every suite in both default and --quiet mode against the
same server. Do not skip this. It is the only thing standing between
this fork and silently breaking every script that parses litmus output.

High value work
===============

These are the things worth doing next, roughly in order of value. They
are bigger than the maintenance items further down.

1. One CLI instead of nine executables
--------------------------------------

Today every suite is its own program: basic.exe, copymove.exe, props.exe
and so on, nine in total, each around 7.9 MiB when statically linked.
That is 71 MiB unpacked, 26 MiB zipped, for what is really one tool, and
means a user has to know which executable to reach for. Almost all of
that is nine copies of the same statically linked OpenSSL, expat and
neon.

Replace them with a single `litmus-cli` taking the suite as a
subcommand:

    litmus-cli basic http://host/dav/
    litmus-cli locks --json http://host/dav/
    litmus-cli list                       # what suites exist
    litmus-cli all --json http://host/dav/

How the code is shaped now, and what has to change:

* Each src/<suite>.c defines its own `ne_test tests[]` and relies on
  the harness in neon/test/common/tests.c providing `main()`. Two
  suites cannot be linked into one binary, because `tests` and `main`
  would collide.
* The fix is to rename each suite's array to `<suite>_tests` (a macro
  per translation unit keeps the diff small), build a registry mapping
  name to array, and turn the harness `main()` into a callable
  `run_suite(const char *name, ne_test *tests, int argc, char **argv)`.
* The harness has file-scope state (`passes`, `fails`, `skipped`,
  `warnings`, `count`, `aborted`, `records`, `nrecords`). Running more
  than one suite in a process means resetting all of it between runs.
  Miss one and `litmus-cli all` reports nonsense on the second suite.
  Reset in one function, next to the declarations, not scattered.
* `litmus_init()` in src/common.c calls `exit()` on a bad command line.
  That is fine for one-shot programs and wrong for a dispatcher.
* src/lockbomb.c is compiled twice with different flags to produce
  lockbomb and lockbomb-single. As subcommands that becomes one
  registration plus a `--threads` option.

Keep single-suite behaviour identical. The regression rule at the top
of this file applies with full force here: same text output, same exit
status (number of failures), same JSON. Prove it by diffing the output
of every suite before and after, in default and --quiet mode, against
the same server. Consider keeping the old executable names as thin
wrappers, or at least document the mapping, so existing scripts do not
break silently.

2. Throughput and latency measurement
-------------------------------------

litmus answers "is this server correct". It cannot answer "is this
server fast", which is the other question every server author has. A
`bench` subcommand would close that gap, and most of the machinery
(sessions, auth, cleanup) already exists.

Scenarios worth having:

* Many small files uploaded in parallel, bounded concurrency.
* The same set downloaded in parallel.
* One large file up, then down.
* Report per scenario: file count, total bytes, wall-clock duration,
  MiB/s, and errors collected but not fatal, so one failed transfer
  does not discard the whole measurement.

Two implementation details that are easy to get wrong and will silently
produce meaningless numbers:

* **Do not upload zeros or a repeating pattern.** Servers, proxies and
  filesystems in the path may compress, deduplicate or sparse-allocate
  it, and the measured rate becomes fiction. Generate one buffer of
  random bytes once, a few MiB, and stream from it at a rotating
  offset. Calling the CSPRNG per read is far too slow to measure a
  network with; repeating a single small block is compressible. Give
  each parallel stream its own starting offset so concurrent uploads
  are not byte-identical.
* **Bound concurrency explicitly**, with a counting semaphore, and make
  the limit an option. Unbounded parallelism measures the client's
  scheduler, not the server.

Latency deserves its own probe, and does not need raw sockets or
administrator rights: connect a TCP socket to the target host and port
N times, timing each, then report min, max, mean, jitter as the mean
absolute deviation from the mean, and loss as failed over attempted.
Pause briefly between attempts so it does not look like a flood. This
matters because it lets an author separate network round-trip time from
server processing time, which is otherwise guesswork when a suite looks
slow.

`hyperfine` is on this machine and is the right tool for measuring the
harness itself, as opposed to the server.

3. Connection and timeout tuning should be visible
--------------------------------------------------

Bulk transfer performance depends on settings that are currently
implicit: idle connection count and lifetime, per-host connection
limits, TLS handshake timeout, response header timeout, the
expect-continue wait, and whether keep-alive is on. neon already reuses
connections well (a props run is 36 requests over one connection), but
a benchmark mode needs these to be deliberate and reportable, otherwise
two runs are not comparable.

Also worth an escape hatch: some servers are markedly worse over HTTP/2
for bulk transfer than over HTTP/1.1. Being able to pin the protocol
version turns "the benchmark is slow" into a specific, reportable
finding.

4. Stable error classification in the JSON
------------------------------------------

`context` is a human-readable string today, so a consumer wanting to
branch on the kind of failure has to pattern-match prose. Add a stable
machine field alongside it: the operation that failed (PUT, GET, MKCOL,
MOVE, DELETE, PROPFIND, LOCK), the HTTP status observed, and the path.
Define the set of kinds once and treat it as part of the JSON contract.
Keep `context` exactly as it is; this is an addition, not a
replacement.

5. Tests for the harness itself
-------------------------------

There are no unit tests. Every check requires a live server, which is
why the wsgidav harness exists. The result accounting, the JSON
emission, the escaping, the notrun bookkeeping and the error paths can
all be tested without a network by substituting a fake transport that
returns canned responses. This is the largest testability gap in the
tree and it is what makes changes to tests.c risky enough to need the
byte-for-byte diffing ritual described above.

A synthetic suite exercising warnings, contexts and every status was
used while building the JSON output and worked well. Make that a
permanent, checked-in test rather than a throwaway.

6. Optional chunked-upload suite
--------------------------------

Several widely deployed DAV servers implement a chunked upload
extension: MKCOL a per-transfer directory, PUT the parts into it under
zero-padded numeric names, then MOVE a virtual assembly path onto the
final destination. It is not in RFC 4918, so it must be opt-in and must
not affect the default suite list or the expected results, but it is
easy to implement wrongly and there is no convenient conformance test
for it.

7. Release and CI improvements
------------------------------

* Add a concurrency group so that pushing several commits in quick
  succession cancels the superseded runs instead of running all of
  them.
* Add dependabot for the actions themselves.
* Publish the executables individually as release assets, not only
  inside the zip, so a download instruction can be a single fixed URL
  that always points at the latest release. Keep the asset names free
  of version strings for that reason; the zip can stay for people who
  want everything at once.
* Build artifacts per job, then collect them in one release job that
  merges them into a single directory before publishing.
* A release step must fail rather than overwrite an existing good
  release.
* Gate the build on a lint job.

Outstanding work
================

Smaller maintenance items, in rough priority order.

* The autotools build relinks every executable on every run. The link
  rules produce targets named "basic", "copymove" and so on, but MinGW
  gcc writes "basic.exe", so make never sees the target as up to date.
  Fixing it means putting $(EXEEXT) through the rules, the ALL_TESTS and
  TESTS lists, the install target and the litmus driver script. It is
  not hard, but it touches enough of the build to need careful testing
  on both Windows and Unix. Makefile.w32 does not have this problem.

* Test failure paths leak. Most tests build URLs with ne_concat and
  return through the ON* macros, which do not free anything. The three
  cases that left files on disk have been fixed (src/basic.c,
  src/lockbomb.c) because those littered the temp directory on every
  failing run. The remaining leaks are memory only, in a process that
  is about to exit, so they are untidy rather than harmful. Cleaning
  them up properly means a cleanup path per test.

* threadfn() in src/lockbomb.c returns early on error without calling
  ne_session_destroy(). Same category as above.

* --trace does not cover the lockbomb worker threads. They create their
  own sessions in threadfn() rather than using i_session, so the trace
  hooks are never attached. Tracing 400,000 lock/unlock round trips
  would not be useful anyway, but writing to one FILE from 20 threads
  would need locking, so this is deliberate. Say so if anyone asks.

* Nothing reports the fork release. The executables have no --version,
  and ./litmus --version prints the upstream "litmus 0.18", so someone
  holding a downloaded binary cannot tell which release it came from.
  Putting the fork version in AC_INIT would fix it and would also show
  up in the User-Agent, which would help server operators, but it means
  bumping configure.ac and regenerating win32/config.h for every
  release, and those two can drift apart. Worth doing properly, with
  the version in one place that both build paths read.

* CI rebuilds the wsgidav virtualenv on every run. Caching it with
  actions/cache would save about half a minute per job. Low value while
  the job is this short.

* Additional test coverage, carried over from the upstream TODO list
  and still unimplemented. Do these here:
    - PROPPATCH may return 207 with an embedded 423; the lock tests
      check for 423 directly. Extending the PROPPATCH interface to
      return an ne_status per operation would let them check properly.
    - lock-null resource tests.
    - collection creation with a %20 space in the name.
  Adding these makes the suite more thorough than the version it was
  forked from, so say that plainly in the README once they land, and
  add them to tests/expected-wsgidav.txt with the new counts.

* Mine the upstream issue tracker and pull requests, including closed
  ones, for test cases and fixes that were proposed but never landed.
  Some contain real bugs worth covering. Take what has technical merit,
  verify each one against a live server before keeping it, and update
  the expected results.

Things that were looked at and deliberately not done
====================================================

* Merging the tolsen fork's tests wholesale, requested in upstream
  issue 8. See the "What was not changed" section of README.md for the
  reasoning: it is 1643 lines with no shared history, stale by many
  commits, and unreviewed. That decision is about taking the whole
  branch in one go, and does not rule out lifting individual test cases
  from it, or from any other upstream issue or pull request, once each
  has been read and verified on its own merits. See the mining item
  above.

* Connection reuse was measured, in case it was a performance problem.
  It is not: a props run makes 36 requests over one TCP connection. The
  slowest tests are copy_coll and move_coll in copymove, at about two
  seconds each, and that time is the server copying a collection, not
  litmus. There is nothing to win on the client side.

* Prefixing message bodies in the --trace output the way request and
  response headers are prefixed. Bodies are dumped by neon inside
  square brackets instead. That is better, not worse: PROPFIND and LOCK
  bodies are XML, so nearly every line starts with "<", which is also
  the response-header prefix. The brackets keep the boundary
  unambiguous.
